WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
It's June 5th, 2026, Friday.

00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:05.120
Let's wrap the week with a story

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.540
that has a real lesson in it.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:10.600
China's one child policy aimed to ease population pressure,

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:14.180
but instead produced a 30 million person gender imbalance

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:15.900
and a shrinking workforce

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.700
that will shape the country for decades.

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:22.100
In the spring of 1983, family planning workers

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:25.020
in rural Sichuan moved from village to village

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:27.100
with quotas and ultrasound machines.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:30.820
They recorded births and enforced the new national limit

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.280
of one child per couple.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:35.400
The measure had been introduced three years earlier

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.700
by senior leaders who believed rapid population growth

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.820
would block economic modernization.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:44.100
Within a generation, the same policy that sought

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.820
to create a smaller, more prosperous population

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:49.040
had produced millions of missing girls.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:52.200
It also created an inverted age structure,

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.840
a cohort of only children emerged

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.860
who social and economic effects are still unfolding.

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:02.600
The policy originated in the late 1970s under Deng Xiaoping.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:05.740
China was emerging from the cultural revolution

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.920
and the chaotic population surges of the Mao era.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:12.240
Between 1949 and 1979,

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:15.880
the population had roughly doubled to nearly one billion.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:19.200
Officials feared that continued high fertility

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:23.240
would overwhelm food supplies, schools, and job creation.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:27.640
A small group of systems analyst, most prominently

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.220
missile scientist Song Jian,

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:33.000
applied cybernetic models borrowed from military planning.

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:37.560
They argued that a strict one-child limit for roughly 20 years

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:42.000
would bring the total population down to an optimal 700 million

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.200
by the middle of the 21st century.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:47.840
At the time, the calculations appeared rigorous.

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:49.800
The trade-off seemed acceptable.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:53.120
Short-term reproductive sacrifice for long-term

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.260
per capita gains in living standards.

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:59.900
Provincial experiments with two child limits in the 1970s

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:02.520
had already shown fertility could be lowered quickly

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:06.200
when local cadres were given clear targets and resources.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:09.820
National leaders therefore viewed the one-child role

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:12.240
not as an experiment in social engineering,

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:15.600
but as a necessary time-limited intervention

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:18.700
supported by the best quantitative tools available.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.300
The policy was formally announced in 1980

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:25.080
and written into the Constitution in 1982.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:28.480
Urban work units and rural production teams

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:30.340
received annual birth quotas.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:34.540
Couples who exceeded them faced fines scaled to local income,

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:37.040
loss of housing and education benefits,

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:41.120
and in some documented cases workplace or administrative pressure

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:42.360
to terminate pregnancies.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:45.060
Early data appeared encouraging.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:48.660
The total fertility rate fell from roughly 2.8

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:53.620
in 1979 to around 1.8 by the mid-1980s.

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:56.060
Official reports credited the policy

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:58.620
with preventing several hundred million births.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:00.840
Implementation was never uniform.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:04.120
World families whose first child was a girl

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:07.320
were often permitted a second birth after a waiting period.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:10.220
Ethnic minorities and some frontier regions

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:11.420
were largely exempt.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.080
By the early 1990s, portable ultrasound machines

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:16.640
had spread to county hospitals.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:19.880
They allowed earlier determination of fetal sex

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:22.260
and accelerated a practice that had previously

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.160
been limited to postnatal selection.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:27.340
The most immediate distortion was a sharp rise

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:29.140
in the recorded sex ratio at birth.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:34.920
By the 2000 census, the national ratio had reached 117 boys

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:36.260
for every 100 girls.

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:39.740
In several provinces, it exceeded 120.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:42.800
Families facing the one child constraint

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:45.520
and still holding a cultural preference for sons

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:47.860
used a new technology of ultrasound

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:51.640
to practice sex selective abortion on a scale large enough

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:54.800
to remove an estimated 30 to 40 million females

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:56.860
from the population over three decades.

00:03:57.740 --> 00:03:59.740
The resulting marriage market imbalance

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:03.280
left millions of men, especially poorer rural men,

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:05.040
unable to form families.

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:07.640
Later studies have linked this imbalance

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:10.260
to higher rates of crime and social instability

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:12.920
in regions with the most skewed cohorts.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:16.640
At the same time, the policy compressed the period

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:19.180
during which large numbers of working age adults

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:22.300
supported a relatively small elderly population.

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:26.440
When the first only child generation reached employment age,

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:29.380
the dependency ratio began to rise rapidly.

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:33.240
By 2020, there were roughly 2.5 workers

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:35.060
for every person over 65.

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:38.520
This marked a reversal of the favorable ratio

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:40.340
that had underpinned earlier growth.

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:43.480
The social experience of singleton children

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:45.440
often called little emperors,

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:48.220
produced measurable differences in personality

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:49.540
and consumption patterns.

00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:52.800
Parents and grandparents concentrated resources

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:54.180
on one offspring.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.420
Yet these micro-level effects were secondary

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:59.440
to the macro-demographic squeeze

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:03.420
because fertility had already been falling before 1980,

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:07.300
isolating the policy's exact contribution remains difficult.

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:11.600
The speed and uniformity of the decline clearly exceeded

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:13.940
what voluntary trends alone would have produced.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:17.720
By the early 2010s, internal government reports

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:20.520
and academic demographers were documenting

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:23.020
the labor force contraction and the growing cost

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:23.860
of elder care.

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:28.180
In 2015, the central government ended the one child limit.

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:31.320
It introduced a universal two-child policy

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:34.080
effective January 1, 2016.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:37.400
Three years later, the rule was further relaxed

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:38.440
to three children.

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:42.080
Birth numbers rose modestly in 2016

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:43.980
and then resumed their decline.

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:47.720
This indicated that economic pressures, housing costs

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:50.420
and changed expectations now outweighed

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:51.820
the earlier legal constraint.

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:55.340
The gender imbalance among young adults persists.

00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:58.040
It will affect marriage and caregiving patterns

00:05:58.040 --> 00:05:59.480
into the 2040s.

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:02.820
Provincial governments have introduced cash subsidies

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:04.620
and extended maternity leave.

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:07.760
Yet none have reversed the underlying age structure

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:10.700
created between 1980 and 2015.

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:14.320
Demographic policies set in motion processes

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:16.560
whose full consequences appear only

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:18.500
after a full generation has passed.

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:21.460
This makes reversal costly and slow.

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:25.600
When a single quantitative target is given enforcement power

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:28.900
over a complex social system, families will adapt

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:31.360
in ways the original models did not anticipate.

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:34.000
This is especially true when technology

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:35.860
supplies new means of compliance.

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:38.520
Any intervention that alters the age

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:40.660
or sex composition of a population

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:43.180
therefore carries compounding fiscal

00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:46.200
and social obligations that extend far beyond

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:47.740
the original planning horizon.

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:51.080
The question for today's policy makers is

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:53.260
which current numerical targets

00:06:53.260 --> 00:06:55.980
whether for carbon, housing, or migration

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:58.640
may similarly lock in imbalances

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:01.380
whose correction will fall to people not yet born.

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:03.520
That wraps today's case.

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:06.040
The lessons travel further when you share them.

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:08.800
Send this episode to someone who'd find it useful.

